---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/essay.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Changelog"
  description="What changed with each edition of Shades of Singularity."
>
  <div class="content-column" style="padding-top: var(--space-xl); padding-bottom: var(--space-lg);">
    <header class="changelog-header">
      <h1>Changelog</h1>
      <p class="changelog-subtitle">What changed with each edition.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="essay-body">
      <section class="changelog-entry">
        <h2>September 2026 edition</h2>
        <p class="changelog-date">Published September 17, 2026.</p>
        <p>
          Two new shades were added and the outcome matrix, several
          essays, and every short essay were updated to reflect the
          events of July through September 2026. The earlier version of
          the site is preserved at the
          <a href="/archive/2026-early/">Early 2026 edition</a>
          and remains reachable from the footer.
        </p>
        <h3>New shades</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="/shades/the-swarm/">The Swarm (#31)</a>: the
            collective-behavior failure mode of merely-capable agents,
            documented at operational scale in the Hugging Face incident
            of July 2026 and in a pattern of related events at
            RubyGems, an abandoned German forum, ten additional sites,
            OpenAI's own supercomputer, Anthropic's evaluations, and
            DeepMind's mathematical research swarm.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/shades/autonomous-lethal-weapons/">Autonomous
            Lethal Weapons (#32)</a>: the removal of the human from the
            kill decision, documented at Zaporizhzhia on July 6, 2026
            and in Ukrainian and Russian drone practice, in a UK
            Ministry of Defence policy review, and in Anthropic's
            threat report on Russian drone designs.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Score revisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>#2 Concentration of AI Power: unmanaged &minus;3 &rarr; &minus;4 (dividend 5 &rarr; 6)</li>
          <li>#6 Cognitive Atrophy Trap: governed +1 &rarr; +2 (dividend 4 &rarr; 5)</li>
          <li>#7 Geopolitical AI Arms Race: likelihood ~80% &rarr; ~90% (moves to Tier 1)</li>
          <li>#13 Financial Chain Reaction: likelihood ~60% &rarr; ~65%</li>
          <li>#14 Alignment Failure: likelihood ~55% &rarr; ~65%</li>
          <li>#21 Intelligence Explosion: likelihood ~25% &rarr; ~35%</li>
          <li>#23 AI-Enabled Bioweapons: summary line rewritten to reflect Anthropic's September 2026 statement that it can no longer rely on a capability gap.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Essays and short essays</h3>
        <p>
          Every full essay and every short essay received a September
          2026 update section. The largest additions were to Essay 6
          (the Hugging Face swarm as investigated, the Astra system
          card and monitorability collapse, the Pacing letter and the
          CEO cascade, the OpenAI disclosure framework, the Coxon
          resignation, and a revised closing that names both
          feasibility-reckoning conditions as met). Essay 1's Acemoglu
          section was updated around the Anthropic Economics Team's
          Working Paper 2026-02. Essay 3's opening was extended with
          the autonomous-weapons developments that overtook the
          Anthropic-Pentagon dispute. Essay 4 added the MIT Ad Hoc
          Committee report and the hollowing-at-the-top pattern.
          Essay 5 added the child-protection regulatory record.
        </p>
        <h3>Why</h3>
        <p>
          Between July and mid-September 2026, the two conditions the
          Early 2026 edition of Essay 6 named for the managed path, a
          visible near-miss and a political realignment, both occurred
          within roughly two weeks of each other. This edition records
          those events, adds two shades for failure modes the earlier
          edition did not have a home for, and revises seven scores.
          The archived edition is kept so that the earlier reasoning
          can be read against what followed.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .changelog-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }
  .changelog-header h1 {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
  }
  .changelog-subtitle {
    color: var(--text-secondary, #6b6b6b);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .changelog-entry h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
  }
  .changelog-entry h3 {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
  }
  .changelog-date {
    color: var(--text-secondary, #6b6b6b);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    margin-top: -0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .changelog-entry ul {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }
  .changelog-entry li {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }
</style>
